-- §3  Every classification the queries make, against the classes it declares.
# One row per (relation, class): the relation that classifies, the type its classes belong to,
# what one counted row is, the class, how many landed in it, and the standing somebody declared
# for it. Every class of every declared type has a row, including the classes nothing landed in.
#
# The reading of the census
#   The arms that drive each type's classes from `enum_range` are in `epistemics/class_cells.sqlc`,
#   which other relations read too, so they are written once. What is here is the reading: the
#   same rows, in the order each type declares its classes, which is the order of the `CASE` in
#   the relation that assigns them.
#
# `subject` is why two numbers in this column do not compare
#   The relations count different things, so `balls` compares down one relation's own rows and
#   nowhere else: a count of computations and a count of rules add up to nothing.
#   `epistemics/class_sets.sqlc` declares what each relation counts, and §3b shows what happens to
#   it under a scope.
#
# An empty class is a referral, and it has an address
#   The classes nothing lands in are printed, and this program asserts nothing about them.
#   `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` is what asserts: every class of every declared type has exactly
#   one declared standing, and that standing agrees with the count in both directions. So a zero
#   a reader sees below stands beside the reason it is a zero, and a zero with no reason fails
#   that law rather than being merely observed here.
-- epistemics/classes.sqlc beside its declared standings, in each type's declared order.
SELECT c.relation AS "relation!", c.codomain AS "codomain!", c.class AS "class!",
       c.subject AS "subject?", c.balls AS "balls!",
       d.standing::text AS "standing?", d.reason AS "reason?"
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/classes.sqlc)
) c
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/class_domain.sqlc)
) d ON d.relation = c.relation AND d.class = c.class
ORDER BY c.relation, c.ord
